            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>On the first day a new product launched, the ad burned through $200 with a CTR of just 0.3%—a reality many TikTok Shop sellers face. The core contradiction during cold launch is tight budgets but an urgent need to find winning creatives quickly. Instead of repeatedly testing blindly in empty ad accounts, build a well-structured creative pool before launching.</p>
<h2>Phase 1: Pre-Launch Preparation Checklist</h2>
<p>Before launching ads, confirm the following preparations are in place:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Roles and responsibilities clarified</strong>: Who is responsible for creative production? Who handles ad uploads and setup? Who monitors performance? Unclear division of labor causes delays during the fast-paced cold launch period</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Creative formats confirmed</strong>: TikTok Shop ads primarily use vertical video (9:16), with 1:1 and 16:9 versions for multi-placement testing. Align specifications with your design/technical team in advance</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Production timeline assessed</strong>: Work backward from your product launch date. Cold launches typically require at least 10-15 test creatives—ensure adequate production and revision time</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: A creative pool isn't about "more is better"—it's about "complete coverage with adequate reserves." With limited resources, prioritize 2-3 comparable creatives per testing direction.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Phase 2: Production Checklist—What to Prepare for Cold Launch</h2>
<p>Rapid consumption and high iteration frequency define cold launches. An adequate creative pool prevents your account from "running out of fuel."</p>
<h3>2.1 Product Showcase (3-5 pieces)</h3>
<p>Product showcases are your foundation—they quickly communicate core selling points.</p>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:<br />
- [ ] Unboxing video (15-30 seconds), highlighting packaging quality and reveal moments<br />
- [ ] Core feature demo (within 15 seconds), focusing on 1-2 strongest selling points<br />
- [ ] Usage scenario display, authentically recreating your target user's typical environment</p>
<h3>2.2 Scenario-Based Planting (3-5 pieces)</h3>
<p>The core of these creatives is "authenticity"—avoid overly polished commercial tones.</p>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:<br />
- [ ] First-person testimonial perspective ("3-week real review," "Roommate recommended it")<br />
- [ ] Daily scenario integration (kitchen, vanity, commute bag)<br />
- [ ] Native or foreign actors on camera (avoid obvious ad feel)<br />
- [ ] Conversational copy adapted to TikTok browsing habits</p>
<h3>2.3 Pain Point Comparison (2-3 pieces)</h3>
<p>Comparison creatives strengthen purchase decisions, especially for products with clear competitors or alternatives.</p>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:<br />
- [ ] "Problem-solution" structure: Show the pain point first, then introduce your product<br />
- [ ] Before/after comparisons (effect/results/convenience)<br />
- [ ] Visual comparisons (fabric texture, capacity, cleaning results)</p>
<h2>Phase 3: Testing Process—4 Steps for Rapid Filtering</h2>
<p>Once creatives are ready, push testing forward through this process:</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Initial Testing</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Run at least 1 ad per creative direction</li>
<li>[ ] Set initial budget at 3-5x your target CPA (starting at $50-100 per ad)</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor CTR, 3-second completion rate, and page dwell time</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 2: 48-Hour Data Filtering</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Pause creatives with CTR below 1%</li>
<li>[ ] Keep creatives with CTR &gt;1.5% or ≥3 conversions for the next phase</li>
<li>[ ] Record initial performance for each direction for later comparison</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 3: Iteration and Expansion</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] For top-performing directions, quickly produce 3-5 derivative versions (different scenes, music, editing rhythm)</li>
<li>[ ] Maintain two lines per direction: "volume drivers" (budget priority) and "test versions" (ongoing exploration)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 4: Creative Pool Archiving</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Build an internal creative library categorized as "winners," "under observation," and "discarded"</li>
<li>[ ] Tag each creative with test date, product, and key metrics</li>
<li>[ ] Keep "backup versions" for each direction to prevent gaps when winning creatives suddenly decline</li>
</ul>
<h2>Phase 4: Common Cold Launch Issues—Troubleshooting Checklist</h2>
<h3>Issue 1: Persistently Low CTR</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check if the first 3 seconds have a hook (suspense, question, counter-intuitive statement)</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm video opening matches thumbnail copy—avoid "clickbait" causing bounces</li>
<li>[ ] Evaluate if product selling points match target user pain points</li>
<li>[ ] Prepare 3-5 new direction creatives as supplements—avoid repeatedly testing the same direction</li>
</ul>
<h3>Issue 2: Too Many Directions, Scattered Account Weight</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Prioritize testing 2-3 directions during cold launch—no more than 5</li>
<li>[ ] Increase budget for positive ROI directions, immediately pause underperformers</li>
<li>[ ] Avoid frequent bid or budget adjustments during the account learning period (typically 3-5 days)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Issue 3: Insufficient Creative Reserves, Running Out Mid-Launch</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Plan weekly creative output rhythm in advance—maintain 10-15 creatives in testing during cold launch</li>
<li>[ ] Establish "rapid response" mechanisms with your design team to support same-day production of hot directions</li>
<li>[ ] Reuse existing creative edits (different subtitles, music, speed changes) to quickly expand testing volume</li>
</ul>
<h2>Key Checklist Recap</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Team roles and technical specifications confirmed</li>
<li>[ ] Product showcases (3-5), scenario-based (3-5), and comparison pieces (2-3) prepared</li>
<li>[ ] Testing process defined with 48-hour initial filtering</li>
<li>[ ] Creative library archiving system established</li>
<li>[ ] Common issue response checklist ready</li>
</ul>
<p>The essence of cold launch is "finding the optimal solution with limited budget." A creative pool isn't built once and forgotten—it's a structure that dynamically adjusts with testing data. Scale winning creatives, replace declining ones, and continuously explore new directions. Once this virtuous cycle is established, CAC gradually decreases and new products can truly survive the cold launch period.</p>
